window.docContents[158].resolve({"/Distance/#section-excluding-short-cycles":{"contents":"A graph has no cycle of length at most k exactly when it contains no copy of C[n]\nfor 3 ≤ n ≤ k, i.e. when it is C[n]-free (in the sense of Free). Mathlib's\ncycleGraph_isContained_iff says that G contains a copy of C[n] (for n ≥ 3)\niff it has a cycle of length n. (Every cycle has length at least 3, so only\nn ≥ 3 matters.) Excluding these copies therefore bounds the girth from below.\n\nlemma le_egirth_of_free {k : ℕ} (h : ∀ n, 3 ≤ n → n ≤ k → C[n].Free G) :\n    k + 1 ≤ G.egirth := by\n  rw [le_egirth]\n  intro a w hw\n  by_contra! hlt\n  have hk : w.length ≤ k := by exact_mod_cast Order.le_of_lt_add_one hlt\n  have h3 := hw.three_le_length\n  exact h _ h3 hk ((cycleGraph_isContained_iff (by grind)).mpr ⟨_, _, hw, rfl⟩)\n\n\nBeing triangle-free is the case n = 3: C[3] is the complete graph on three\nvertices (cycleGraph_three_eq_top), and Mathlib's cliqueFree_iff_free_top_fin\nidentifies G.CliqueFree n with being K[n]-free.\n\nlemma cliqueFree_three_iff_free_cycleGraph : G.CliqueFree 3 ↔ C[3].Free G := by\n  rw [cycleGraph_three_eq_top, cliqueFree_iff_free_top_fin]\n\n\nSo a triangle-free graph has girth at least 4, and if it is also C[4]-free then\nits girth is at least 5.\n\nlemma four_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three (h : G.CliqueFree 3) : 4 ≤ G.egirth :=\n  le_egirth_of_free fun n h3 hn => by\n    obtain rfl : n = 3 := by grind\n    exact cliqueFree_three_iff_free_cycleGraph.mp h\n\n\nlemma five_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three_of_free (h3 : G.CliqueFree 3)\n    (h4 : C[4].Free G) : 5 ≤ G.egirth :=\n  le_egirth_of_free fun n h3n hn => by\n    obtain rfl | rfl : n = 3 ∨ n = 4 := by grind\n    exacts [cliqueFree_three_iff_free_cycleGraph.mp h3, h4]\n\n\nA graph G is C[4].Free precisely when any two distinct vertices have at most one\ncommon neighbour. If distinct vertices x and y had two distinct common neighbours\nu and v, then x, u, y and v would be four distinct vertices (adjacent\nvertices are distinct), so ![x, u, y, v] would be a copy of C[4]. Conversely, a\ncopy f of C[4] gives distinct vertices f 0 and f 2 with two distinct common\nneighbours f 1 and f 3.\n\nlemma cycleGraph_four_free_iff_common_neighbor_unique :\n    C[4].Free G ↔\n      ∀ x y u v, G.Adj x u → G.Adj u y → G.Adj x v → G.Adj v y → x ≠ y → u = v := by\n  refine ⟨fun hf x y u v hxu huy hxv hvy hxy => ?_, fun h => ?_⟩\n  · by_contra huv\n    have hinj : Function.Injective ![x, u, y, v] := by\n      intro i j hij\n      fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;> simp_all\n    refine hf ⟨⟨⟨![x, u, y, v], fun {i j} hij => ?_⟩, hinj⟩⟩\n    fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;>\n      first | exact absurd hij (by decide) | simp [*, Adj.symm]\n  · rintro ⟨f⟩\n    have hadj {i j : Fin 4} (ha : C[4].Adj i j) : G.Adj (f i) (f j) :=\n      f.toHom.map_adj ha\n    have heq := h (f 0) (f 2) (f 1) (f 3) (hadj (by decide))\n      (hadj (by decide)) (hadj (by decide)) (hadj (by decide))\n      (fun h => absurd (f.injective h) (by decide))\n    exact absurd (f.injective heq) (by decide)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Girth","header":"11.4.1. Excluding Short Cycles","id":"/Distance/#section-excluding-short-cycles"},"/Introduction/#section-how-this-guide-is-structured":{"contents":"This guide is focused entirely on SimpleGraph. We start with the basic definitions\nin the next chapter before moving on to give many different concrete examples in\nConstructions. The following chapter,\nDecidability, Finiteness and\nDegrees, shows how to compute with graphs and introduces vertex degrees.\n\nThe rest of the guide introduces topics mainly according to dependencies in Mathlib:\nMaps, Cliques, Colorings, Walks,\nSubgraphs,\nConnectivity, Distance,\nAlgebraic Graph Theory, before looking\nat some of the classical Theorems about graphs in\nMathlib.\n\nAfter Maps the guide splits into two strands that can be read\nin either order: Cliques and\nColorings on the one hand, and\nWalks on the other. The strands meet in\nSubgraphs, and from there each chapter builds on the\nprevious ones.\n\nMost chapters begin with a few basic sections before moving on to more involved\nmaterial. Sections or subsections whose titles are marked with a star are more\nadvanced. Later sections sometimes use their results, and recall what they need when\nthey do.\n\nA route through the guide\n\nWe use three running examples throughout: the n-dimensional hypercube Q[n]\n(Hypercube), the Mycielskian mμ(G) of a graph G\n(Mycielskian), and the infinite path pathNat on ℕ\n(Constructing a SimpleGraph).\n\nExamples of more advanced topics that we cover in the guide include:\n\n* Graphic Sequences* Independence Number of the\nKneser Graph* Colouring Mycielskians* Greedy Colouring* Pósa Rotations* Bondy–Chvátal Theorem* De Bruijn–Erdős Theorem* Vertex Connectivity* Brooks' Theorem* Cut Vertices and Blocks* Moore and Cage Bounds* Albertson's Precolouring\nExtension Theorem* Counting Copies* The Automorphism Group of the\nHypercube* Kőnig's Theorem* Füredi's Stability Theorem* Zykov's Theorem* Ruzsa–Szemerédi Theorem\n\nTheorems from Mathlib that we survey, mostly in the final chapter\nTheorems, include:\n\n* Hall's Marriage Theorem* Tutte's Theorem* Turán's Theorem and\nMantel's Theorem* Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós Theorem* Erdős–Stone Theorem* The Laplacian* Strongly Regular Graphs* Szemerédi Regularity Lemma* Triangle Removal Lemma and\nRoth's Theorem* Kőnig's Lemma and Ends\n\nEach chapter (except this one) has some exercises with worked solutions. Each set of\nexercises is given first with sorry, and the solutions follow.\n\nSince we want to develop results for our running examples some of these exercises are\nnamed lemmas or theorems rather than anonymous examples. To avoid naming clashes we\nadd a prime (foo') to the versions with sorry.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Introduction","header":"1.4. How This Guide is Structured","id":"/Introduction/#section-how-this-guide-is-structured"}});